  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`, so the query ran without errors. I couldn't confirm that the query actually picks up the 5xx records. The API key variable was empty in my shell, so I had no direct access to the data. All the work was done through the Kibana tool connection.\n\n**1. Fields:** I listed the data view's fields. The data uses `timestamp` as its time field and holds one record per web request: the response code (`response` / `response.keyword`, stored as text like \"503\"), bytes sent, client IP, requested URL, source and destination country (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`), operating system, browser (`agent`) and tags.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-27c760f4 Web logs overview\":** it has 8 panels and is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-27c760f4-web-logs-overview-cae716282085\n- Three single-number panels: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), and total bytes served.\n- A line chart of requests over time.\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of response code share.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and bytes for each.\n- A bar chart of requests by source country.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-27c760f4 5xx spike\":** an Elasticsearch query rule with id `facf2fc1-e2c9-4343-b261-bd8ef35240c4`.\n- It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It counts records whose response code starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes, using `timestamp`.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n\nThe rule has no notification action, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana and nobody is notified. If you want messages, say which channel (for example Slack or email) and I'll set up the connection and attach it to the rule.",
